"sync package - sync - Go Packages (https://pkg.go.dev/sync)\nciteturn18view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://pkg.go.dev/sync\",\"pattern\":\"added in go1.25.0\"}); Total lines: 947\nL447: cite60†Map.Load , cite61†Map.LoadAndDelete , cite62†Map.LoadOrStore , cite65†Map.Swap , cite58†Map.CompareAndSwap , and cite57†Map.CompareAndDelete are read operations; cite59†Map.Delete , cite61†Map.LoadAndDelete , cite64†Map.Store , and cite65†Map.Swap are write operations; cite62†Map.LoadOrStore is a write operation when it returns loaded set to false; cite58†Map.CompareAndSwap is a write operation when it returns swapped set to true; and cite57†Map.CompareAndDelete is a write operation when it returns deleted set to true.\nL448: #### func (*Map) cite105†Clear†cs.opensource.google cite56†¶ added in go1.23.0\nL449: \nL450:     func (m *cite55†Map ) Clear()\nL451: \nL452: Clear deletes all the entries, resulting in an empty Map.\nL453: #### func (*Map) cite106†CompareAndDelete†cs.opensource.google cite57†¶ added in go1.20\nL454: \nL455:     func (m *cite55†Map ) CompareAndDelete(key, old cite93†any ) (deleted cite107†bool )\nL456: \nL457: CompareAndDelete deletes the entry for key if its value is equal to old. The old value must be of a comparable type.\nL458: \nL459: If there is no current value for key in the map, CompareAndDelete returns false (even if the old value is the nil interface value).\nL460: #### func (*Map) cite108†CompareAndSwap†cs.opensource.google cite58†¶ added in go1.20\nL461: \nL462:     func (m *cite55†Map ) CompareAndSwap(key, old, new cite93†any ) (swapped cite107†bool )\nL463: \nL464: CompareAndSwap swaps the old and new values for key if the value stored in the map is equal to old. The old value must be of a comparable type.\nL465: #### func (*Map) cite109†Delete†cs.opensource.google cite59†¶ added in go1.9\nL466: \nL467:     func (m *cite55†Map ) Delete(key cite93†any )\nL468: \nL469: Delete deletes the value for a key. If the key is not in the map, Delete does nothing.\nL470: \nL471: #### func (*Map) cite110†Load†cs.opensource.google cite60†¶ added in go1.9\nL472: \nL473:     func (m *cite55†Map ) Load(key cite93†any ) (value cite93†any , ok cite107†bool )\nL474: \nL475: Load returns the value stored in the map for a key, or nil if no value is present. The ok result indicates whether value was found in the map.\nL476: #### func (*Map) cite111†LoadAndDelete†cs.opensource.google cite61†¶ added in go1.15\nL477: \nL478:     func (m *cite55†Map ) LoadAndDelete(key cite93†any ) (value cite93†any , loaded cite107†bool )\nL479: \nL480: LoadAndDelete deletes the value for a key, returning the previous value if any. The loaded result reports whether the key was present.\nL481: #### func (*Map) cite112†LoadOrStore†cs.opensource.google cite62†¶ added in go1.9\nL482: \nL483:     func (m *cite55†Map ) LoadOrStore(key, value cite93†any ) (actual cite93†any , loaded cite107†bool )\nL484: \nL485: LoadOrStore returns the existing value for the key if present. Otherwise, it stores and returns the given value. The loaded result is true if the value was loaded, false if stored.\nL486: #### func (*Map) cite113†Range†cs.opensource.google cite63†¶ added in go1.9\nL487: \nL488:     func (m *cite55†Map ) Range(f func(key, value cite93†any ) cite107†bool )\nL489: \nL490: Range calls f sequentially for each key and value present in the map. If f returns false, range stops the iteration.\nL491: Range does not necessarily correspond to any consistent snapshot of the Map's contents: no key will be visited more than once, but if the value for any key is stored or deleted concurrently (including by f), Range may reflect any mapping for that key from any point during the Range call. Range does not block other methods on the receiver; even f itself may call any method on m.\nL492: \nL493: Range may be O(N) with the number of elements in the map even if f returns false after a constant number of calls.\nL494: #### func (*Map) cite114†Store†cs.opensource.google cite64†¶ added in go1.9\nL495: \nL496:     func (m *cite55†Map ) Store(key, value cite93†any )\nL497: \nL498: Store sets the value for a key.\nL499: \nL500: #### func (*Map) cite115†Swap†cs.opensource.google cite65†¶ added in go1.20\nL501: \nL502:     func (m *cite55†Map ) Swap(key, value cite93†any ) (previous cite93†any , loaded cite107†bool )\nL503: \nL504: Swap swaps the value for a key and returns the previous value if any. The loaded result reports whether the key was present.\nL505: #### type cite116†Mutex†cs.opensource.google cite66†¶ L506: \nL507:     type Mutex struct {\nL508:     \t// contains filtered or unexported fields\nL509:     }\nL510: \nL511: A Mutex is a mutual exclusion lock. The zero value for a Mutex is an unlocked mutex.\nL512: \nL513: A Mutex must not be copied after first use.\nL514: In the terminology of cite96†the Go memory model†go.dev , the n'th call to cite69†Mutex.Unlock “synchronizes before” the m'th call to cite67†Mutex.Lock for any n < m. A successful call to cite68†Mutex.TryLock is equivalent to a call to Lock. A failed call to TryLock does not establish any “synchronizes before” relation at all.\nL515: #### func (*Mutex) cite117†Lock†cs.opensource.google cite67†¶ L516: \nL517:     func (m *cite66†Mutex ) Lock()\nL518: \nL519: Lock locks m. If the lock is already in use, the calling goroutine blocks until the mutex is available.\nL520: #### func (*Mutex) cite118†TryLock†cs.opensource.google cite68†¶ added in go1.18\nL521: \nL522:     func (m *cite66†Mutex ) TryLock() cite107†bool L523: \nL524: TryLock tries to lock m and reports whether it succeeded.\nL525: \nL526: Note that while correct uses of TryLock do exist, they are rare, and use of TryLock is often a sign of a deeper problem in a particular use of mutexes.\nL527: #### func (*Mutex) cite119†Unlock†cs.opensource.google cite69†¶ L528: \nL595: #### type cite122†Pool†cs.opensource.google cite72†¶ added in go1.3\nL596: \nL597:     type Pool struct {\nL598: \nL599:     \t// New optionally specifies a function to generate\nL600:     \t// a value when Get would otherwise return nil.\nL601:     \t// It may not be changed concurrently with calls to Get.\nL602:     \tNew func() cite93†any L603:     \t// contains filtered or unexported fields\nL604:     }\nL605: \nL606: A Pool is a set of temporary objects that may be individually saved and retrieved.\nL666: #### func (*Pool) cite123†Get†cs.opensource.google cite73†¶ added in go1.3\nL667: \nL668:     func (p *cite72†Pool ) Get() cite93†any L669: \nL670: Get selects an arbitrary item from the cite72†Pool , removes it from the Pool, and returns it to the caller. Get may choose to ignore the pool and treat it as empty. Callers should not assume any relation between values passed to cite74†Pool.Put and the values returned by Get.\nL671: \nL672: If Get would otherwise return nil and p.New is non-nil, Get returns the result of calling p.New.\nL673: #### func (*Pool) cite124†Put†cs.opensource.google cite74†¶ added in go1.3\nL674: \nL675:     func (p *cite72†Pool ) Put(x cite93†any )\nL676: \nL677: Put adds x to the pool.\nL711: #### func (*RWMutex) cite132†TryLock†cs.opensource.google cite80†¶ added in go1.18\nL712: \nL713:     func (rw *cite75†RWMutex ) TryLock() cite107†bool L714: \nL715: TryLock tries to lock rw for writing and reports whether it succeeded.\nL716: \nL717: Note that while correct uses of TryLock do exist, they are rare, and use of TryLock is often a sign of a deeper problem in a particular use of mutexes.\nL718: #### func (*RWMutex) cite133†TryRLock†cs.opensource.google cite81†¶ added in go1.18\nL719: \nL720:     func (rw *cite75†RWMutex ) TryRLock() cite107†bool L721: \nL722: TryRLock tries to lock rw for reading and reports whether it succeeded.\nL723: \nL724: Note that while correct uses of TryRLock do exist, they are rare, and use of TryRLock is often a sign of a deeper problem in a particular use of mutexes.\nL863: #### func (*WaitGroup) cite139†Go†cs.opensource.google cite86†¶ added in go1.25.0\nL864: \nL865:     func (wg *cite83†WaitGroup ) Go(f func())\nL866: \nL867: Go calls f in a new goroutine and adds that task to the cite83†WaitGroup . When f returns, the task is removed from the WaitGroup.\nL868: \nL869: The function f must not panic.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsync package - sync - Go Packages (https://pkg.go.dev/sync)\nciteturn18view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://pkg.go.dev/sync\",\"pattern\":\"must happen before a Wait\"}); Total lines: 947\nL852: Note that calls with a positive delta that occur when the counter is zero must happen before a Wait. Calls with a negative delta, or calls with a positive delta that start when the counter is greater than zero, may happen at any time. Typically this means the calls to Add should execute before the statement creating the goroutine or other event to be waited for. If a WaitGroup is reused to wait for several independent sets of events, new Add calls must happen after all previous Wait calls have returned.\nL854: #### func (*WaitGroup) cite138†Done†cs.opensource.google cite85†¶ L855: \nL856:     func (wg *cite83†WaitGroup ) Done()\nL857: \nL858: Done decrements the cite83†WaitGroup task counter by one. It is equivalent to Add(-1).\nL859: \nL860: Callers should prefer cite86†WaitGroup.Go .\nL861: \nL862: In the terminology of cite96†the Go memory model†go.dev , a call to Done \"synchronizes before\" the return of any Wait call that it unblocks.\nL863: #### func (*WaitGroup) cite139†Go†cs.opensource.google cite86†¶ added in go1.25.0\nL864: \nL865:     func (wg *cite83†WaitGroup ) Go(f func())\nL866: \nL867: Go calls f in a new goroutine and adds that task to the cite83†WaitGroup . When f returns, the task is removed from the WaitGroup.\nL868: \nL869: The function f must not panic.\nL870: If the WaitGroup is empty, Go must happen before a cite87†WaitGroup.Wait . Typically, this simply means Go is called to start tasks before Wait is called. If the WaitGroup is not empty, Go may happen at any time. This means a goroutine started by Go may itself call Go. If a WaitGroup is reused to wait for several independent sets of tasks, new Go calls must happen after all previous Wait calls have returned.\nL871: In the terminology of cite96†the Go memory model†go.dev , the return from f \"synchronizes before\" the return of any Wait call that it unblocks.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nerrgroup package - golang.org/x/sync/errgroup - Go Packages (https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/sync/errgroup)\nciteturn18view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/sync/errgroup\",\"pattern\":\"blocks until\"}); Total lines: 518\nL433: #### func (*Group) cite63†Go†cs.opensource.google cite49†¶ L434: \nL435:     func (g *cite47†Group ) Go(f func() cite64†error )\nL436: \nL437: Go calls the given function in a new goroutine.\nL438: \nL439: The first call to Go must happen before a Wait. It blocks until the new goroutine can be added without the number of goroutines in the group exceeding the configured limit.\nL440: \nL441: The first goroutine in the group that returns a non-nil error will cancel the associated Context, if any. The error will be returned by Wait.\nL442: #### func (*Group) cite65†SetLimit†cs.opensource.google cite50†¶ L443: \nL444:     func (g *cite47†Group ) SetLimit(n cite66†int )\nL445: \nL446: SetLimit limits the number of active goroutines in this group to at most n. A negative value indicates no limit. A limit of zero will prevent any new goroutines from being added.\nL447: \nL448: Any subsequent call to the Go method will block until it can add an active goroutine without exceeding the configured limit.\nL449: \nL450: The limit must not be modified while any goroutines in the group are active.\nL451: #### func (*Group) cite67†TryGo†cs.opensource.google cite51†¶ L452: \nL453:     func (g *cite47†Group ) TryGo(f func() cite64†error ) cite68†bool L454: \nL455: TryGo calls the given function in a new goroutine only if the number of active goroutines in the group is currently below the configured limit.\nL456: \nL457: The return value reports whether the goroutine was started.\nL458: #### func (*Group) cite69†Wait†cs.opensource.google cite52†¶ L459: \nL460:     func (g *cite47†Group ) Wait() cite64†error L461: \nL462: Wait blocks until all function calls from the Go method have returned, then returns the first non-nil error (if any) from them.\nL463: ## cite70†Image Source Files cite53†¶ L464: \nL465: cite71†View all Source files†cs.opensource.google L466: \nL467:   * cite72†errgroup.go†cs.opensource.google L468: \nL469: Click to show internal directories.\nL470: \nL471: Click to hide internal directories.\nL472: \nL473: cite73†Why Go†go.dev cite4†Use Cases†go.dev cite3†Case Studies†go.dev L474: \nL475: cite74†Get Started†learn.go.dev cite75†Playground†play.golang.org cite76†Tour†tour.golang.org cite77†Stack Overflow†stackoverflow.com cite17†Help†go.dev L476: \nL477: cite12†Packages cite9†Standard Library cite78†Sub-repositories cite79†About Go Packages cite11†pkg.go.dev API --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Go Memory Model - The Go Programming Language (https://go.dev/ref/mem)\nciteturn18view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://go.dev/ref/mem\",\"pattern\":\"Atomic Values\"}); Total lines: 534\nL332: ### Atomic Values\nL333: \nL334: The APIs in the cite19†`sync/atomic` package are collectively “atomic operations” that can be used to synchronize the execution of different goroutines. If the effect of an atomic operation A is observed by atomic operation B, then A is synchronized before B. All the atomic operations executed in a program behave as though executed in some sequentially consistent order.\n"
